Hi there!
In the last two chapters we built cat and head.
Both tools share a comfortable property: they read data from the beginning and stop when they have enough. cat stops at EOF. head stops after N lines or bytes.
Both move in one direction: forward.
tail breaks that assumption.
Its job is to show the last N lines of a file. That creates a problem we have not faced before.
To know what the last lines are, you need to know where the file ends.
And to know where the file ends, you need to have reached it.
With head, we could stop early. With tail, we cannot.

Rethinking tail
Most people think of tail as the opposite of head.
That is a useful starting point, but it misses the interesting part.
head gives you a prefix of the stream. tail gives you a suffix.
Getting a prefix is easy: start reading and stop after N units.
Getting a suffix is fundamentally harder, because you cannot know where the suffix begins until you know where the data ends.
This asymmetry shapes every design decision tail makes.
There are two ways to solve this problem, and both are legitimate. Which one is correct depends on something the program often cannot know in advance: whether the input is a regular file or a stream.

The Challenge: Files vs Streams
Before writing any code, we need to understand why tail has two modes of
operation, even if it never exposes that distinction to the user.
A regular file has a known size. The operating system knows where it starts
and where it ends. You can ask the kernel to jump to any position inside it. You can read backwards. You can seek.
A stream; a pipe, a terminal, standard input has none of those properties.
Data arrives sequentially. You cannot rewind. You cannot know the size in advance.
This distinction matters because tail must handle both:
tail file.txt # regular file: we can seek
cat file.txt | tail # stream: we cannot seekThe first case allows an efficient solution. The second forces a different approach.
Understanding why leads to a much deeper understanding of Unix I/O.

Approach 1: The Universal Method (Reading Everything)
The first approach works on any input: files, pipes, terminals, network streams.
The idea is simple.
Instead of trying to find where the last N lines begin, we read the entire input from start to finish, keeping only the last N lines in memory at any time.
When the input ends, we print whatever we kept.
The Circular Buffer
To keep only the last N lines without knowing in advance how many lines there are, we use a data structure called a circular buffer.
A circular buffer is an array treated as if its end wraps around to its beginning.
Imagine an array of N slots. As lines arrive, we fill slot 0, then slot 1, and so
on. When we reach the last slot and a new line arrives, we do not stop, we
overwrite slot 0 again. Then slot 1. And so on.
At any point, the array holds the N most recently seen lines. Older lines are
overwritten as new ones arrive.
This is exactly what we need.
When the input ends, the circular buffer contains the last N lines. We print them in order, accounting for the wraparound.
Minimal Implementation
Let us start with a version that reads from standard input and stores the last
ten lines.
#include <unistd.h>#define BUFFER_SIZE 4096
#define MAX_LINES 10
#define MAX_LINE_LEN 4096
int main(void)
{
char lines[MAX_LINES][MAX_LINE_LEN];
char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
ssize_t bytes_read;
int head;
int total;
int line_pos;
int i;
head = 0;
total = 0;
line_pos = 0;
while ((bytes_read = read(0, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE)) > 0)
{
for (i = 0; i < bytes_read; i++)
{
lines[head][line_pos] = buffer[i];
line_pos++;
if (buffer[i] == '\n' || line_pos == MAX_LINE_LEN - 1)
{
lines[head][line_pos] = '\0';
head = (head + 1) % MAX_LINES;
total++;
line_pos = 0;
}
}
}
/* If the last line had no trailing newline, flush it */
if (line_pos > 0)
{
lines[head][line_pos] = '\0';
head = (head + 1) % MAX_LINES;
total++;
}
/* Print in correct order */
{
int count = (total < MAX_LINES) ? total : MAX_LINES;
int start = (total < MAX_LINES) ? 0 : head;
int j;
for (j = 0; j < count; j++)
{
int idx = (start + j) % MAX_LINES;
int k = 0;
while (lines[idx][k])
k++;
write(1, lines[idx], k);
}
}
return 0;
}
How the Circular Buffer Works Here
The variable head always points to the slot where the next line will be written.
When head reaches MAX_LINES, the expression (head + 1) % MAX_LINES wraps it back to 0. This is the core of the circular buffer.
When we are done reading, the oldest line we kept is at position head itself
(the slot that would be overwritten next). We print from there, wrapping around, for a total of count lines.
If the file had fewer than MAX_LINES lines, start is simply 0 and we print
everything from the beginning.
Known Limitations of This Version
This implementation has two honest constraints worth naming:
Fixed line length. Any line longer than MAX_LINE_LEN — 1 bytes gets split
across two slots. The real tail handles lines of arbitrary length using dynamic memory allocation. For now, we accept the limit and document it.
Fixed number of lines. The -n flag is not yet supported. We will add it shortly.

Extended Version: Supporting -n, -c, and Multiple Files
Let us now extend the implementation with flag support and file arguments.
We will keep -c (byte mode) in the circular approach as well, using a simpler byte-level circular buffer.
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>#define BUFFER_SIZE 4096
#define MAX_LINES 10000
#define MAX_LINE_LEN 4096
/* ── helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
int is_numeric(char *s)
{
if (!s || !*s)
return 0;
while (*s)
{
if (*s < '0' || *s > '9')
return 0;
s++;
}
return 1;
}
int to_number(char *s)
{
int res = 0;
while (*s >= '0' && *s <= '9')
{
res = res * 10 + (*s - '0');
s++;
}
return res;
}
int str_len(char *s)
{
int n = 0;
while (s && s[n]) n++;
return n;
}
/* ── circular buffer: lines ──────────────────────────────────── */
void tail_lines_universal(int fd, int n)
{
char lines[MAX_LINES][MAX_LINE_LEN];
char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
ssize_t bytes_read;
int head;
int total;
int line_pos;
int i;
head = 0;
total = 0;
line_pos = 0;
while ((bytes_read = read(fd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE)) > 0)
{
for (i = 0; i < bytes_read; i++)
{
lines[head % n][line_pos] = buffer[i];
line_pos++;
if (buffer[i] == '\n' || line_pos == MAX_LINE_LEN - 1)
{
lines[head % n][line_pos] = '\0';
head++;
total++;
line_pos = 0;
}
}
}
if (bytes_read < 0)
{
write(2, "tail: read error\n", 17);
return;
}
if (line_pos > 0)
{
lines[head % n][line_pos] = '\0';
head++;
total++;
}
{
int count = (total < n) ? total : n;
int start = (total < n) ? 0 : head - count;
int j;
for (j = 0; j < count; j++)
{
int idx = (start + j) % n;
write(1, lines[idx], str_len(lines[idx]));
}
}
}
/* ── circular buffer: bytes ──────────────────────────────────── */
void tail_bytes_universal(int fd, int n)
{
char ring[BUFFER_SIZE * 4];
char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
ssize_t bytes_read;
int ring_size;
int head;
int total;
int i;
ring_size = n < (int)sizeof(ring) ? n : (int)sizeof(ring);
head = 0;
total = 0;
while ((bytes_read = read(fd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE)) > 0)
{
for (i = 0; i < bytes_read; i++)
{
ring[head % ring_size] = buffer[i];
head++;
total++;
}
}
if (bytes_read < 0)
{
write(2, "tail: read error\n", 17);
return;
}
{
int count = (total < ring_size) ? total : ring_size;
int start = (total < ring_size) ? 0 : head - count;
int j;
char c;
for (j = 0; j < count; j++)
{
c = ring[(start + j) % ring_size];
write(1, &c, 1);
}
}
}
/* ── flag parsing ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
int parse_flag(int argc, char **argv, int *mode, int *limit)
{
*mode = 0; /* 0 = lines, 1 = bytes */
*limit = 10;
if (argc <= 1 || argv[1][0] != '-')
return 1;
if (argv[1][1] == 'n')
{
if (argc < 3 || !is_numeric(argv[2]))
{
write(2, "tail: invalid line count\n", 25);
return -1;
}
*limit = to_number(argv[2]);
return 3;
}
if (argv[1][1] == 'c')
{
if (argc < 3 || !is_numeric(argv[2]))
{
write(2, "tail: invalid byte count\n", 25);
return -1;
}
*mode = 1;
*limit = to_number(argv[2]);
return 3;
}
if (is_numeric(&argv[1][1]))
{
*limit = to_number(&argv[1][1]);
return 2;
}
write(2, "tail: invalid option\n", 21);
return -1;
}
/* ── main ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
int main(int argc, char **argv)
{
int fd;
int i;
int mode;
int limit;
int arg_index = parse_flag(argc, argv, &mode, &limit);
if (arg_index < 0)
return 1;
i = arg_index;
if (i >= argc)
{
if (mode == 1)
tail_bytes_universal(0, limit);
else
tail_lines_universal(0, limit);
return 0;
}
for (; i < argc; i++)
{
fd = open(argv[i], O_RDONLY);
if (fd == -1)
{
write(2, "tail: cannot open ", 18);
write(2, argv[i], str_len(argv[i]));
write(2, "\n", 1);
continue;
}
if (mode == 1)
tail_bytes_universal(fd, limit);
else
tail_lines_universal(fd, limit);
close(fd);
}
return 0;
}
This version works. Test it against the real tail on any file.
But it has a cost that becomes visible on large files.
To show the last 10 lines of a 2GB log file, this implementation reads all 2GB.
It does not store all of it, the circular buffer discards old data, but it
still has to read every byte before it can print anything.
On most modern systems, this is fast enough not to matter for everyday use.
But it is not elegant. And it is not how the real tail works on regular files.
There is a better way, one that relies on a system call we have not used yet.

Approach 2: The Seekable Method (Reading from the Bottom)
For regular files, the operating system gives us something streams do not have: the ability to move the read position anywhere in the file.
That system call is lseek.
Understanding lseek
off_t lseek(int fd, off_t offset, int whence);lseek moves the current read position of a file descriptor.
offset is how far to move, in bytes.
whence is the reference point:
- SEEK_SET: move to offset bytes from the beginning of the file
- SEEK_CUR: move to offset bytes from the current position
- SEEK_END: move to offset bytes from the END of the file
The return value is the new absolute position in the file. This is useful: calling lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_END) jumps to the end and also tells you the total file size.
off_t size = lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_END); /* size = file size in bytes */lseek only works on regular files. If you call it on a pipe or terminal, it
returns -1 and sets errno to ESPIPE. This is exactly the boundary between
Approach 1 and Approach 2.

The Byte Case: Elegant and Immediate
For -c N, the seekable solution is almost trivial.
We know the file size. We know we want the last N bytes. We jump directly to (size — N) and stream from there to the end.
void tail_bytes_seekable(int fd, int n)
{
char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
ssize_t bytes_read;
off_t size; size = lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_END);
if (size < 0)
{
write(2, "tail: cannot seek\n", 18);
return;
}
if (n >= size)
lseek(fd, 0, SEEK_SET); /* want more than the file has */
else
lseek(fd, -n, SEEK_END); /* jump to (size - n) */
while ((bytes_read = read(fd, buffer, BUFFER_SIZE)) > 0)
write(1, buffer, bytes_read);
if (bytes_read < 0)
write(2, "tail: read error\n", 17);
}
No circular buffer. No full read. For a 2GB file with -c 100, this reads
exactly 100 bytes.

The Line Case: Reading Backwards
Lines are harder, because we do not know their lengths in advance.
There is no index that maps “line 7” to a byte offset. The file is just bytes.
To find where the last N lines begin, we have to count newlines and to count them efficiently, we should count them starting from the end.
The strategy:
1. Jump to the end of the file.
2. Read backward in chunks, scanning each chunk for newline characters.
3. Count newlines from the end. When we have found N of them, we know where the last N lines begin.
4. Seek to that position and stream forward.
Reading backward means repeatedly seeking to a position before the current one and reading a chunk from there.
void tail_lines_seekable(int fd, int n)
{
char buffer[BUFFER_SIZE];
ssize_t bytes_re
